  "answer_text": "The name **Catherine McCoy** (including its common alternative spelling, **Katherine McCoy**) primarily refers to a few notable individuals across design, law, and literature:\n\n### 1. Katherine McCoy (Graphic Designer & Educator)\nIf you are looking up a prominent figure in the professional and academic world, you are likely thinking of **Katherine McCoy** (often searched as \"Catherine\"). \n* **Who she is:** A highly influential American graphic designer and educator born in 1945. \n* **Why she is famous:** Alongside her husband, Michael McCoy, she co-chaired the graduate Design department at the **Cranbrook Academy of Art** for 24 years (1971\u20131995). Under her leadership, Cranbrook became a legendary hub for design experimentation, shifting from High Modernism to postmodernism and deconstructivism.\n* **Awards:** She was awarded the prestigious **AIGA Medal** in 1999 for her immense contributions to design education and practice.\n\n### 2. Catherine McCoy (Plaintiff in Donald Trump / ACN Lawsuit)\nIf you saw the name in political or legal news, she is one of the lead plaintiffs in a high-profile civil lawsuit.\n* **Who she is:** She, along with several other plaintiffs, filed a class-action lawsuit against Donald Trump, his children, and his business empire. \n* **The Case:** The plaintiffs alleged that Trump promoted and endorsed **ACN Opportunity, LLC** (a multi-level marketing/pyramid scheme) on his television show, *The Celebrity Apprentice*, and at live events. McCoy and others alleged they were conned into losing thousands of dollars under the false promise of a \"get-rich-quick\" business opportunity. The lawsuit gained significant media attention when Trump and his family were deposed in 2022.\n\n### 3. Catherine McCoy (Fictional Character in *The Great Gatsby* Retellings)\nIn F. Scott Fitzgerald's classic novel *The Great Gatsby*, Myrtle Wilson's sister is a minor character simply named **Catherine**. \n* In modern literature\u2014most notably Jillian Cantor\u2019s 2022 novel ***Beautiful Little Fools*** (a retelling of *The Great Gatsby* from the perspective of its female characters)\u2014she is given the full name **Catherine McCoy** and is reimagined as a central protagonist and a suffragette. \n\n### 4. Catherine McCoy (Longshorewoman & Retaliation Plaintiff)\n* **Who she is:** A third-generation dockworker on the California waterfront.\n* **Why she is in the news:** In 1998, McCoy and five other African-American female dockworkers filed a discrimination lawsuit against the Pacific Maritime Association (PMA). After settling out of court, McCoy faced severe workplace retaliation. She sued again and made legal headlines in 2008 when a jury awarded her **$1.2 million** in damages for workplace retaliation.",
